---
import Site from "../layouts/Site.astro";
import { readManifest } from "../lib/releases.mjs";
const release = await readManifest();
---

<Site
  title="Build and run workflows on your own machine"
  description="Connect scripts, APIs, containers, and AI agents in Runstead's visual editor. Schedule workflows and inspect the output of each step."
>
  <div class="wrap home">
    <section class="hero" aria-labelledby="hero-title">
      <p class="eyebrow dot">
        {release
          ? `Public beta · ${release.version}`
          : "Public beta coming soon"}
      </p>
      <h1 id="hero-title">Build and run workflows on your own machine.</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        Connect scripts, APIs, containers, and AI agents in a desktop app. Build the
        workflow visually, schedule it, and inspect the output of each step.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button" href={release ? "/download/" : "#film"}>
          {release ? "Download Runstead" : "Watch the film"}
          <span aria-hidden="true">{release ? "↗" : "↓"}</span>
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/getting-started/">
          Read the setup guide →
        </a>
      </div>
      <p class="fine">First release for macOS 13 or later.</p>
    </section>

    <section class="promo" id="film" aria-labelledby="film-title">
      <div class="promo-heading">
        <p class="eyebrow">Meet Runstead</p>
        <h2 id="film-title">Serious workflows. Seriously simple.</h2>
        <p>Build visually. Run on your terms. With or without code.</p>
      </div>
      <figure class="promo-film">
        <video
          controls
          playsinline
          preload="none"
          poster="/videos/runstead-film.webp"
          width="1920"
          height="1080"
          aria-label="Runstead product film, 60 seconds"
          aria-describedby="film-caption"
        >
          <source src="/videos/runstead-film-v2.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
          <p><a href="/videos/runstead-film-v2.mp4">Watch the Runstead film.</a></p>
        </video>
        <figcaption id="film-caption">
          <span>60 seconds · Original music · Sound on</span>
          <span>Illustrative product demo</span>
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
      <div class="promo-extras">
        <details class="promo-short">
          <summary>Watch the 30-second version</summary>
          <video
            controls
            playsinline
            preload="none"
            poster="/videos/runstead-social.webp"
            width="1080"
            height="1920"
            aria-label="Runstead short film, 30 seconds, portrait"
          >
            <source src="/videos/runstead-social.mp4" type="video/mp4" />
            <p><a href="/videos/runstead-social.mp4">Watch the short film.</a></p>
          </video>
        </details>
        <details class="promo-transcript">
          <summary>Read the video transcript</summary>
          <div>
            <p>
              <strong>Serious workflows. Seriously simple.</strong> For builders,
              with or without code. Runstead: build visually, run on your terms.
              Instrumental electronic music plays throughout; there is no narration.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Build a workflow without writing code.</strong> In the visual
              editor, customer feedback flows from a connected API to an AI
              decision. The question “Does this feedback need a reply?” creates
              the next path: draft a reply, then ask a person to approve it.
              Select Weekdays at 9:00, review, and start the run. All four steps
              complete on your machine. This illustrative demo uses tools
              configured in advance.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Your tools. One flow.</strong> Connect AI harnesses such as
              Codex, Claude Code, and Gemini; Docker images; commands; remote
              servers over SSH; decision APIs; and imported OpenAPI actions.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Runs locally. Works together.</strong> The full film shows
              team members running workflows, viewing results, and managing
              workflows through MCP. Share access through MCP and REST while
              keeping execution on your machine.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Or simply ask.</strong> A chat agent connected through MCP
              receives the request “Build a daily feedback workflow. Flag urgent
              issues for review.” Runstead creates, validates, and schedules the
              workflow for review. Build, run, inspect, and fix workflows from
              the conversation.
            </p>
            <p>
              <strong>Easy to build. Yours to run.</strong> For the team that
              imagines it and the people who make it happen. The short film ends
              with “Runs on your Mac. Shared with your team.” Explore Runstead at
              runstead.dev.
            </p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </section>

    <figure class="product-preview">
      <a
        href="/images/workflow-builder.jpg"
        aria-label="Open the workflow editor screenshot at full size"
      >
        <img
          src="/images/workflow-builder.jpg"
          alt="Runstead workflow editor with a daily report workflow and the selected step's settings"
          width="1869"
          height="1012"
          fetchpriority="high"
        />
      </a>
      <figcaption>
        <span>Connect steps on the canvas. Select a step to edit its settings.</span>
        <span>Development preview · Open image to enlarge</span>
      </figcaption>
    </figure>

    <section
      class="section report-example"
      id="example"
      aria-labelledby="example-title"
    >
      <div class="example-copy">
        <p class="eyebrow">Example workflow</p>
        <h2 id="example-title">Turn a CSV into a daily report.</h2>
        <p>
          Run a Python script against sample sales data, save a Markdown report,
          and check the result in the run log.
        </p>
        <ol class="example-steps">
          <li>
            <strong>Read orders</strong>
            <span>Read the sample orders from a CSV.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Build report</strong>
            <span>Calculate revenue and average order value.</span>
          </li>
          <li>
            <strong>Save report</strong>
            <span>Write the report and print its contents.</span>
          </li>
        </ol>
        <a
          class="text-link"
          href="/docs/workflow-builder/#example-build-a-daily-report"
        >
          Build this workflow →
        </a>
      </div>
      <figure class="product-preview run-preview">
        <a
          href="/images/workflow-run.jpg"
          aria-label="Open the completed workflow run screenshot at full size"
        >
          <img
            src="/images/workflow-run.jpg"
            alt="Completed daily report workflow in Runstead with the report visible in the selected step's output"
            width="1345"
            height="886"
            loading="lazy"
          />
        </a>
        <figcaption>
          Sample data · 3 orders · $2,450.00 revenue. Open image to enlarge.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>
    </section>

    <section class="section operations" aria-labelledby="api-title">
      <article>
        <h2 id="api-title">Use API operations as workflow steps.</h2>
        <p>
          Import an OpenAPI spec and choose an operation by name. Runstead
          builds its request form from the spec. Reuse the connection across
          workflows in the project.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/apis/">Import an API →</a>
      </article>
      <ol class="example-steps">
        <li>
          <strong>Import the spec</strong>
          <span>Upload JSON or YAML, paste it, or enter its URL.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Configure the request</strong>
          <span>Fill in inputs and connect a project secret if authentication is needed.</span>
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Use the response</strong>
          <span>Pass response fields into a script, agent, or another API step.</span>
        </li>
      </ol>
    </section>

    <section class="section operations" aria-label="Scheduling and debugging">
      <article>
        <h2>Schedule a workflow.</h2>
        <p>
          Run on demand, daily, on weekdays, or on a custom schedule. On macOS,
          Runstead keeps working from the menu bar when you close its window.
          Scheduled runs need an awake machine with your user signed in.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/scheduling/">How schedules work →</a>
      </article>
      <article>
        <h2>Inspect a failed step.</h2>
        <p>
          Open a run to see which steps finished and which failed. Select a step
          to read its output and errors. Workflow definitions and execution logs
          are stored on your machine.
        </p>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/workflow-builder/#check-run-and-schedule">
          Run and inspect a workflow →
        </a>
      </article>
    </section>

    <aside class="connections" aria-label="Sharing and integrations">
      <p>
        <strong>Share a project.</strong> Export a project and import it on
        another device. Connect clients to a running Runstead host through
        MCP or REST, using API keys.
      </p>
      <div>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/sharing/">Project sharing →</a>
        <a class="text-link" href="/docs/mcp/">MCP and API access →</a>
      </div>
    </aside>

    <section class="closing" aria-labelledby="pricing-title">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Planned launch pricing</p>
        <h2 id="pricing-title">Free for one project.</h2>
        <p>
          No Runstead account required. Pro supports up to ten projects for
          $15/month (USD).
          <a href="/pricing/">Compare plans →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="availability">
        <a class="button" href="/download/">
          {release ? "Download Runstead" : "Check release availability"}
          <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span>
        </a>
        <p class="fine">
          {release ? "Public beta available" : "Public beta coming soon"}{" "}
          · macOS 13+
        </p>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>
</Site>
